  "type": "multipleCorrect",
  "level": 3,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Two non-conducting solid spheres of radii&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></math>, having uniform volume charge densities <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> and <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>, respectively, touch each other. The net electric field at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></math> from the centre of the smaller sphere, along the line joining the centres of the spheres, is zero. The ratio <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math> can be</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "izR2w",
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mo>-</mo> <mn>4</mn> </mrow> </math>",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "sFr7s",
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mo>-</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mn>2</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mn>5</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "8859O",
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mn>2</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mn>5</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "T0EWY",
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mn>4</mn> </mrow> </math>",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/f388598d-0252-482e-8fd8-e898914b8cb8-Screenshotfrom2020-08-0508-56-08.png\" style=\"width: 321px; height: 181px;\" />Field due to small sphere<br /><math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>K</mtext> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>4</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mi>&#960;</mi> <msup> <mtext>R</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mtext>R</mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mn>2</mn> </msup> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br />Field due to bigger sphere<br /><math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>K</mtext> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>4</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mn>&#960;</mn> <msup> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mtext>R</mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mn>3</mn> </msup> <mo>&#215;</mo> <mtext>R</mtext> </mrow> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mtext>R</mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mn>3</mn> </msup> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br />The net electric field at point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>&nbsp;will be</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mi>net</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>Equating above two, we get <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>=</mo> <mn>4</mn> </mrow> </math><br />[Charges cannot be of an opposite sign otherwise field will not zero]<br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/da647bd4-36ed-4b0d-a365-5d1b6d99a6a8-Screenshotfrom2020-08-0509-00-40.png\" style=\"width: 324px; height: 172px;\" /></p><p>Similarly, the net electric field at point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;becomes zero. Then <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#961;</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>=</mo> <mo>-</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mn>2</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mn>5</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math> here both spheres should have opposite polarity.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
